lashNexusByteOrder\00Loading DCM Flash Image from file: [%s]\00File size does not match flash size. Probaly not a legitimate image. [File Size: %u, Flash Size: %u]\00All bytes were not read properly! [Bytes Read: %u/%u]\00Read %d bytes.\00Root Block does not contain the proper format sequence!\00Dumping VMU Filesystem\00%-20s: %40d\00Blocks Used\00Free\00File Count\00Failed to retrieve VMS header of file? [dirEntry type: %d, dirEntry size: %d, first block: %d]\00[%d] - %s\00VMU Flash - Root [block: %d]\00Root.formattedSegment[%d] = %d (should be %d)\00Ok\00Unformatted\00Formatted\00Use Custom Color\00Color\00<%d, %d, %d, %d>\00%u/%u/%u%u (%s) %u:%u:%u\00Date Formatted\00Total Size\00Partition\00Root Block\00Fat Block\00Fat Size\00Directory Block\00Directory Size\00Save Area Block\00Save Area Size\00Icon Shape\00User Size\00Execution File\00Unknown Value: Root[%u] = %x\00Fat Entries\00FREE\00EOF\00DMG\00[%03u] = % 4s \00Loading .VMU/.BIN Flash image from file: [%s]\00Loading ICONDATA_VMS File [%s].\00An ICONDATA.VMS file is already present in flash. [File Index: %d, First Block: %d, Size: %d]\00Could not determine file size! [0 bytes]\00Could not actually read entirety of file, but continuing anyway [%d/%d].\00fileProperties && byteSize\00gyVmuFlashNewFilePropertiesFromIconDataVms\00Could not retrieve FAT entry for FAT block: %d\00Could not retrieve FAT entry for Root block: %d\00Couldn\'t find fat entry [%d] when allocating directory!\00but >= 0 && but < VMU_GAMEPAD_MAX\00../../source/gyro_vmu_gamepad.c\00gyVmuButtonStateSet\00NOP\00Stalls processor for one clock-cycle.\00BR r8\00Branch unconditionally. The target address is specified using an 8-bit relative address. The signed 8-bit offset is added to the address of the instruction following the BR. No PSW flags are affected.\00LD d9\00Load the operand into the ACC register. No PSW flags are affected.\00LD @Ri\00CALL a12\00Call function. The entry address of the function is specified using a 12-bit absolute address, so the upper 4 bits of this address must be the same as for the instruction following the CALL. The return address (the address of the instruction following the CALL instruction) is pushed on the stack. The lower 8 bits of the address are pushed first, then the upper 8 bits. No PSW flags are affected.\00CALLR r16\00Call function. The entry address of the function is specified using a 16-bit relative address. The unsigned 16-bit offset is added to the address of the instruction following the CALLR minus one to produce the target address. The addition is performed modulo 65536, which makes it possible to call a lower address as well. The return address (the address of the instruction following the CALLR instruction) is pushed on the stack. The lower 8 bits of the address are pushed first, then the upper 8 bits. No PSW flags are affected.\00BRF r16\00Branch unconditionally. The target address is specified using a 16-bit relative address. The unsigned 16-bit offset is added to the address of the instruction following the BRF minus one to produce the target address. The addition is performed modulo 65536, which makes it possible to branch to a lower address as well. No PSW flags are affected.\00ST d9\00Store the contents of the ACC register into the operand address. No PSW flags are affected.\00ST @Ri\00CALLF a16\00Call function. The entry address of the function is specified using a full 16-bit absolute address. The return address (the address of the instruction following the CALLF instruction) is pushed on the stack. The lower 8 bits of the address are pushed first, then the upper 8 bits. No PSW flags are affected.\00JMPF a16\00Jump unconditionally. The target address is specified using a full 16-bit absolute address. No PSW flags are affected.\00MOV #i8, d9\00Set the contents of the operand to a constant value. No PSW flags are affected.\00MOV #i8, @Rj\00JMP a12\00Jump unconditionally. The target address is specified using a 12-bit absolute address, so the upper 4 bits of this address must be the same as for the instruction following the JMP. No PSW flags are affected.\00MUL\00Perform a multiplication. The ACC and C registers together form a 16-bit operand (ACC being the high 8 bits, and C being the low 8 bits) which is multiplied by the contents of the B register. The result is a 24-bit number that is stored in the ACC, C and B registers (the high 8 bits are stored in B, the middle 8 bits in ACC, and the low 8 bits in C). CY is cleared, and OV is set if the result is greater than 16 bits, otherwise cleared. AC is not affected.\00BE #i8, r8\00Branch if the contents of the ACC register (or the indirect operand in the third form above) are equal to the immediate or direct operand. See BR for address calculation. Additionally, CY is set to 1 if ACC (or the indirect operand) is strictly less than the immediate or direct operand. AC and OV are unaffected.\00BE d9, r8\00BE @Rj, #i8, r8\00DIV\00Perform a division. The ACC and C registers together form a 16-bit operand (ACC being the high 8 bits, and C being the low 8 bits) which is divided by the contents of the B register. The result is a 16-bit quotient that is stored in ACC and C (the high 8 bits in ACC, and the low 8 bits in C), and an 8-bit remainder that is stored in B. CY is cleared, and OV is set if the remainder is zero, otherwise cleared. AC is not affected.\00BNE #i8, r8\00Branch if the contents of the ACC register (or the indirect operand in the third form above) are not equal to the immediate or direct operand. See BR for address calculation. Additionally, CY is set to 1 if ACC (or the indirect operand) is strictly less than the immediate or direct operand. AC and OV are unaffected.\00BNE d9, r8\00BNE @Rj, #i8, r8\00BPC d9, b3, r8\00If the specified bit of the operand is set, clear the bit and branch. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00LDF\00Load a constant from Flash space into the ACC register. The Flash address is formed by taking the TRH and TRL registers viewed as a 16-bit value (TRH being the upper 8 bits, and TRL being the lower 8 bits). No PSW flags are affected.\00STF\00Write to flash some fucking how.\00DBNZ d9, r8\00Decrement the operand by one, and branch if the result is not zero. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00DBNZ @Ri, r8\00PUSH d9\00Push the operand on the stack. The SP register is first incremented by one, and the operand value is then stored at the resulting stack position. No PSW flags are affected.\00INC d9\00Increment the operand by one. No PSW flags are affected.\00INC @Ri\00BP d9, b3, r8\00Branch if the specified bit of the operand is set. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00POP d9\00Pop the operand from the stack. The value is read from the stack position pointed out by the current value of the SP register, and SP is then decremented by one. No PSW flags are affected.\00DEC d9\00Decrement the operand by one. No PSW flags are affected.\00DEC @Ri\00BZ r8\00Branch if the ACC register is zero. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00ADD #i8\00Add the operand to the ACC register. CY, AC and OV are set according to the result.\00ADD d9\00ADD @Ri\00BN d9, b3, r8\00Branch if the specified bit of the operand is not set. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00BNZ r8\00Branch if the ACC register is not zero. See BR for address calculation. No PSW flags are affected.\00ADDC #i8\00Add the operand and the carry bit (CY) to the ACC register. CY, AC and OV are set according to the result.\00ADDC d9\00ADDC @Ri\00RET\00Return from function. The PC register is popped from the stack. The upper 8 bits are popped first, then the lower 8 bits. No PSW flags are affected.\00SUB #i8\00Subtract the operand from the ACC register. CY, AC and OV are set according to the result.\00SUB d9\00SUB @Ri\00NOT1 d9, b3\00Invert the specified bit in the operand. No PSW flags are affected.\00RETI\00Return from interrupt. The PC register is popped from the stack. The upper 8 bits are popped first, then the lower 8 bits. No PSW flags are affected.\00SUBC #i8\00Subtract the operand and the carry bit (CY) from the ACC register. CY, AC and OV are set according to the result.\00SUBC d9\00SUBC @Ri\00ROR\00Rotate the contents of the ACC register one bit to the right. The least signigicant bit will wrap immediately around to the most signigicant bit. No PSW flags are affected.\00LDC\00Load a constant from ROM space into the ACC register. The ROM address is formed by adding the old value of ACC to the contents of the TRH and TRL registers viewed as a 16-bit value (TRH being the upper 8 bits, and TRL being the lower 8 bits). No PSW flags are affected.\00XCH d9\00Exchange the contents of the operand with the contents of the ACC register. No PSW flags are affected.\00XCH @Ri\00CLR1 d9, b3\00Clear the specified bit in the operand. No PSW flags are affected.\00RORC\00Rotate the contents of the ACC register one bit to the right. The least signigicant bit is copied to the CY flag, and the old value of CY will be place in the most signigicant bit. The AC and OV flags are unaffected.\00OR #i8\00Perform bitwise OR between the operand and the ACC register. No PSW flags are affected.\00OR d9\00ROL\00Rotate the contents of the ACC register one bit to the left. The most signigicant bit will wrap immediately around to the least signigicant bit. No PSW flags are affected.\00AND #i8\00Perform bitwise AND between the operand and the ACC register. No PSW flags are affected.\00AND d9\00AND @Ri\00SET1 d9, b3\00Set the specified bit in the operand. No PSW flags are affected.\00ROLC\00Rotate the contents of the ACC register one bit to the left. The most signigicant bit is copied to the CY flag, and the old value of CY will be place in the least signigicant bit. The AC and OV flags are unaffected.\00XOR #i8\00Perform bitwise XOR between the operand and the ACC register. No PSW flags are affected.\00XOR d9\00XOR @Ri\00../